findings.what_breaks:
`sig_fixed_weight` silently drops a ticker missing (or NaN) from the panel and renormalises the rest to gross 1.0 (line 329). `fixed_weight_next` then compares the drifted weights against that RENORMALISED target:

    if is_rebalance_day and any(
            abs(drifted.get(t, 0.0) - target.get(t, 0.0)) > band
            for t in set(drifted) | set(target)):

`set(drifted) | set(target)` includes the vanished sleeve, whose target is now 0.0 and whose drifted weight is still ~0.20, so |0.20 - 0.0| = 0.20 > band=0.05 is a *guaranteed* breach, and the renormalised cash leg (0.40 -> 0.50) is a second one. The helper's fail-closed branch (line 350, `cash is not None and cash not in target`) only protects the ONE ticker passed as `cash`; a missing G-sec, gold or Nifty sleeve produces no warning at all. The result is a real booked trade — the entire sleeve sold, cost charged — caused purely by a data gap, on a day with zero actual drift. It round-trips when the ticker reappears.

This contradicts the committed hypothesis: docs/capital_engine/hypotheses/A1_shield_core.md §3 says "trade only when a sleeve's weight drifts more than ±5 pp absolute from target" and §1 says the point is "keeping turnover, and therefore cost and tax, near zero"; it also contradicts the helper's own docstring ("weights that merely drift are not trades") and the M1 review record's claim that the protection is "Fail-closed if the cash ETF is missing" — which is the only sleeve that fails closed. Whichever sleeve is silently dropped, the ledger still records the contestant as shield_core 40/20/20/20 while it actually runs 50/25/25.

